Package: MEXGB
Type: Package
Title: Mixed-Effect Extreme Gradient Boosting for Longitudinal Data
Version: 0.5.0
Authors@R: c(person("Ajibade Kunle", "Saheed", role = "aut"),
             person("Oyebayo Ridwan", "Olaniran", role = c("aut", "cre"),
                    email = "olaniran.or@kwasu.edu.ng"),
             person("Mohd Asrul Affendi", "Abdullah", role = "aut"))
Maintainer: Oyebayo Ridwan Olaniran <olaniran.or@kwasu.edu.ng>
Description: Extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost) is a regularised, second-order (Newton)
    tree-boosting method, but like other tree ensembles it treats clustered observations as
    independent. This package implements Mixed-Effect Extreme Gradient Boosting ('MEXGB'),
    which embeds subject-specific random effects within the 'xgboost' tree-boosting framework
    for high-dimensional longitudinal and clustered data with continuous (Gaussian), binary
    (Bernoulli) or right-censored time-to-event (frailty Cox) outcomes. The fixed effect is fitted by 'xgboost' and the random effects and variance
    components by an Expectation-Maximization / Best Linear Unbiased Prediction loop, extended to
    binary and survival outcomes by penalised quasi-likelihood. The implementation mirrors the 'MEGB' package,
    replacing first-order gradient boosting ('gbm') by extreme gradient boosting ('xgboost').
